{
  "id": "ep081",
  "duration": 45.698,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.687,
      "text": "Sunlight feels instant, but its energy spent 100,000 years escaping the core.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The Sun by the Atmospheric Imaging Assembly of NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory - 20100819.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/SDO (AIA)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sun_by_the_Atmospheric_Imaging_Assembly_of_NASA%27s_Solar_Dynamics_Observatory_-_20100819.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.833,
          "end": 5.7,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.687,
      "end": 12.132,
      "text": "When nuclear fusion occurs deep inside the Sun, it releases tiny light particles called photons.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Proton\u2013proton chain reaction.png",
        "credit": "Nagendra Nath Mondal",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proton%E2%80%93proton_chain_reaction.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.7,
          "end": 12.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.132,
      "end": 19.152,
      "text": "These photons bounce constantly between dense particles in an epic and incredibly long obstacle course.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Proton\u2013proton chain reaction.png",
        "credit": "Nagendra Nath Mondal",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proton%E2%80%93proton_chain_reaction.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.133,
          "end": 19.167,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 19.152,
      "end": 26.547,
      "text": "At the very same exact time, nuclear reactions also release nearly massless ghost particles called neutrinos.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "FirstNeutrinoEventAnnotated.jpg",
        "credit": "Argonne National Laboratory",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FirstNeutrinoEventAnnotated.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 19.167,
          "end": 26.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.547,
      "end": 32.567,
      "text": "Because they interact so weakly with normal matter, neutrinos escape the dense core right away.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Borexino schematic.jpg",
        "credit": "Marco G. Giammarchi",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borexino_schematic.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 26.533,
          "end": 32.567,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 32.567,
      "end": 40.242,
      "text": "Detecting those mysterious neutrinos today proved the Sun produces the same energy now as it did long ago.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Neutrino detector - National Museum of Nature and Science, Tokyo - DSC07827.JPG",
        "credit": "Daderot",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neutrino_detector_-_National_Museum_of_Nature_and_Science,_Tokyo_-_DSC07827.JPG"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 32.567,
          "end": 40.233,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 40.242,
      "end": 45.348,
      "text": "Sunlight hitting your face today is actually an ancient relic spent escaping the core.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The Sun by the Atmospheric Imaging Assembly of NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory - 20100819.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/SDO (AIA)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sun_by_the_Atmospheric_Imaging_Assembly_of_NASA%27s_Solar_Dynamics_Observatory_-_20100819.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 40.233,
          "end": 45.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
